            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "CaraLF"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Yummy and oh so easy. The only thing I would add to the instructions is to cook the stems first, then add the leaves a few minutes later. That way, the stems aren't over-cooking while you are waiting for the stems to get cooked."
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Denise Dunning Hougland"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This was a strech for me as I have never ventured into bok choy before. I used a nonstick pan and about a tablespoon of olive oil. Followed the suggestions cooking the white pieces with the garlic which I grated over the top, first. I also grated in a bit of fresh ginger. When they were softened I added the greens and let them steam uncovered until wilted. Stiring occasionally. I sprinkled some sea salt on when served. The results were great. Light, crunchy, quite tasty and my husband agreed. We will do this again. Thanks so much!",
            "datePublished": "2012-06-07T18:34:53.630Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "DREGINEK"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Classic and simple side; great for any Asian themed main dish. I used regular bok choy and started with some sliced mushrooms and the white parts first, adding the green parts the last few minutes. Not fancy or in-your-face but overall, very good. Thanks Frantic Cook!",
            "datePublished": "2024-09-09T00:10:40.327Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "pj"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Though I'm sure I've had Bok Choy in Asian food many times, I never had it alone till recently at a dinner party. The cook made it basically the same way as this recipe, except for adding some sliced fresh ginger. \n\nI followed the method in this recipe, using a large head of Bok Choy, and added:\nA few Tbsp thinly sliced crystallized ginger\n3 big garlic cloves, thinly sliced\n1/4 medium onion thinly sliced\nA few shakes reduced sodium soy sauce\ni sautéed the above together, then tossed in:\na few handfuls of fresh spinach leaves, sliced into strips. \nand as it was looking a bit dry, I added:\nAbout 1/4 c broth from a Chinese restaurant\n Delicious! The photo was taken after it sat on the stove for awhile, so it doesn't look as fresh and green as it had been",
            "datePublished": "2018-12-05T01:24:18.250Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Juanita Galbreath"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I browned garlic in olive oil then added baby Bok choy with 2 tablespoons of Soy Sauce. I skipped the salt and it had a perfect flavor.",
            "datePublished": "2025-06-30T19:12:56.170Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "naples34102"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This is a perfectly written recipe. Easy execution and just a few, basic ingredients lend an elegant simplicity I appreciate. I used grapeseed oil rather than vegetable oil, basically because I had it on hand to use. I also did not cook the garlic in the oil (never do). I added it to the bok choy about half way through the cooking time. I'm happy to be the first to review this delicious and dependable side dish which I served with Asian Grilled Chicken and Classic Fried Rice, recipes also from this site.",
            "datePublished": "2012-12-04T17:23:04.820Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "YellowCocoa6307"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Baby bok choy is the way to go... so tender &amp; tasty - I added sautéed shallot, mushrooms &amp; garlic, as well as some julienned carrots. Will have this for dinner a couple times with either shrimp or dumplings. Yum!! 😋",
            "datePublished": "2025-02-17T18:53:27.896Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Flora Gee"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Great introduction to cooking book choy. I have always enjoyed eating bok choy out but decided I should learn to make it at home as another healthy green. Loved it! Made exactly as recipe was written but added two tablespoons water to steam the greens at the end. Will be serving it over rice as a side with fish tonight",
            "datePublished": "2026-07-11T18:40:37.393Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "mjmanch"
            },
            "reviewBody": "We used full-size bok choy, as the store had no baby bok choy available. This was a side veggie at Thanksgiving, and it was a big hit. I cooked it in a wok and allowed it to steam covered most of the cooking time. Simple and delicious.",
            "datePublished": "2012-11-22T22:57:56.207Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
